      "title": "Police use facial recognition to scan one million faces in WA trial leading to 122 arrests",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/02/wa-police-facial-recognition-technology-trial",
      "description": "<p>Deployments of surveillance vans over five months result in nine false alerts and led officers to engage with 146 registered sex offenders</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>More than a million people have had their faces scanned in a trial of real-time facial recognition technology in Western Australia, resulting in 122 arrests and nine incorrect alerts.</p><p>WA police said under the five-month trial, which concluded on Saturday, the technology scanned 1,380,882 faces after 100 deployments across 43 regional and metropolitan locations.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/02/wa-police-facial-recognition-technology-trial\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-02T02:02:34"
    },
    {
      "title": "Pauline Hanson and James Ashby rule out running against Nationals leader Matt Canavan in Capricornia",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/02/nationals-leader-matt-canavan-one-nation-james-ashby-capricornia-election-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>‘My political career may die,’ senator says after Michelle Landry announced she will not recontest her Queensland seat of Capricornia</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily political email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>The Nationals leader, Matt Canavan, will run for a lower house Queensland seat at the next federal election but will avoid having to face off against a key One Nation figure.</p><p>Canavan confirmed his bid following the announcement that Capricornia MP Michelle Landry will retire, leading to speculation it could be a tight race with Pauline Hanson’s national director.</p><p><strong><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/aug/28/politics-really-newsletter-guardian-australia-free-best-daily-auspol-email-subscribe?CMP=copyembed\">Sign up for Guardian Australia’s Politics, really newsletter here</a></strong></p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/nov/02/nationals-leader-matt-canavan-one-nation-james-ashby-capricornia-election-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
